{"id": 92, "ts": 1783900786.4734342, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I remain convinced that **Candidate\u202fD** is the safer overall choice. While the panel has highlighted Candidate\u202fC\u2019s impressive technical and cognitive abilities\u2014anticipating danger, seeing complex connections, and excellent spatial vision\u2014I must point out that we still have **no evidence of C\u2019s responsibility or adaptability**, and his difficulty taking criticism indicates a weak feedback orientation. Those gaps are critical for long\u2011haul operations where a pilot must continuously adapt to evolving procedures and respond constructively to crew input.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fD brings concrete, documented strengths: he **takes responsibility seriously**, **concentrates very well**, **solves problems extremely well**, and **responds adequately to unexpected events**. These observations directly support operational reliability, stress resilience, and decision quality\u2014core safety criteria for international flights. Although D\u2019s hot temper and arrogant, know\u2011it\u2011all attitude are noted, they are comparable to C\u2019s show\u2011off behavior and, importantly, D\u2019s proven responsibility and problem\u2011solving capacity suggest he can mitigate those interpersonal issues with proper crew\u2011resource training.\nGiven the lack of essential evidence for C and the solid, safety\u2011critical record for D, I keep my provisional recommendation for **Candidate\u202fD**."}
